  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Anticipate cooler temperatures and periods of precipitation\n   this week.\n\n\nCurrent satellite and radar is showing the low pressure system\nstarting to move onshore over Washington this morning. This system\nwill continue to move eastward bringing widespread precipitation \nto the Northern Rockies later today and overnight. Snow levels \ntonight are expected to lower to around 4000-5000 feet allowing \nfor accumulating snow in the higher terrain. The latest model \nguidance is indicating the heaviest snow will be across the \nMission mountains up into Glacier National Park by Tuesday \nafternoon. \n\nA secondary disturbance will re-enforce the trough of low pressure\nover the Pacific Northwest on Wednesday. Then by the end of the\nweek another disturbance moves over the state of Washington. This\nlatter disturbance appears to cause southwesterly flow to develop\nover the Northern Rockies allowing snow levels to rise as warmer\nair moves into the region on Friday. The longer term models are\nstarting to trend towards this low moving over the region during\nthe weekend causing lowering of snow levels along with another\nround of widespread precipitation.",
